  "narrative": "No evidence assessments are due at this explore stage. The supported core in this dataset is now fairly stable: feature_037=1 is robustly associated with stronger composite dependency; feature_010=1 and feature_002=1 are robustly associated with weaker dependency; feature_027=1 is robustly associated with stronger dependency, including validation in a clean triple-strong-absent stratum; and feature_063=1 is supported primarily in the clean validated triple-strong-absent context. Broad or sparse feature_063 variants and feature_003 remain near-threshold and should not be repeatedly mined unless a comparison has adequate cells and a clear threshold-exceeding interval. The new proposals therefore focus on completing robustness and independence checks for the core markers, plus a small number of heterogeneity probes.\n\nFirst, I complete the median-split clinical-course pattern for the central markers. H97 already tested feature_027 in the triple-strong-absent stratum restricted to higher log_pfs_months and met the threshold, but the lower-log_pfs half remains untested. I propose feature_027 in feature_037=0, feature_010=0, feature_002=0 with feature_040<=median. The expected raw feature_027=1 minus 0 contrast is negative, i.e. stronger dependency, so the claim direction is -1 and anticipated raw direction is -1. This is a parented refinement of H97. Alternatives include attenuation or nullity if the feature_027 association is concentrated in one clinical-course stratum. Similarly, H65-H67 accepted lower-log_pfs estimates for feature_037, feature_010, and feature_002 in other-marker-absent strata. I propose direct higher-log_pfs estimates for those three predictors in the corresponding absence strata. Expected raw contrasts are negative for feature_037 (stronger dependency) and positive for feature_010 and feature_002 (weaker dependency). These comparisons complete the low/high log_pfs split for the dominant markers. Earlier interaction tests against the high-log_pfs subgroup did not support threshold interactions, so persistence is likely, but direct estimates are needed to interpret conditional stability.\n\nSecond, I test whether the accepted feature_027 and feature_063 clean-stratum associations are separable from the near-threshold feature_003 signal. I propose feature_063 in the triple-strong-absent, feature_027=0, feature_003=0 stratum, a stricter version of H89, and feature_027 in the triple-strong-absent, feature_063=0, feature_003=0 stratum, a stricter version of H91. The expected raw contrasts remain negative for both feature_063 and feature_027 because both are hypothesized to mark stronger dependency. If their lower bounds remain above 0.15 with adequate cells, this supports dataset-specific separability from feature_003; if intervals narrow or cross threshold, the accepted claims should remain interpreted as clean-stratum conditionals rather than broadly general marker effects. This avoids further chasing feature_003 itself, which remains unresolved.\n\nThird, I add two heterogeneity probes that do not require new near-threshold marker claims. The first tests whether a high research-signature level on feature_038 identifies a larger feature_037 stronger-dependency effect. The direction is set to -1 to test whether the feature_037 association is amplified in the feature_038>=0.75 subgroup relative to its complement, but the anticipated raw interaction is uncertain. The second tests whether feature_037's association varies between feature_024 level_002 and its complement. feature_024 has two common nominal levels and adequate cells; H36 showed no marginal association, making it a useful negative-candidate heterogeneity variable. Both interaction claims should be interpreted only as dataset-specific contrasts among opaque nominal or constructed assay labels, not as biological mechanism.\n\nThe existing active probes H103-H109 remain useful: feature_034 level_003 versus level_002 adds broad categorical coverage; H104-H106 test whether feature_037, feature_010, and feature_002 retain their associations within feature_027=1; and H107-H109 add common predictor probes. The new proposals are higher priority if they have adequate cells because they complete the dominant-marker clinical-course pattern and test exact separability from feature_003, while the heterogeneity probes guard against over-assuming additivity. All proposed comparisons remain observational, and conditioning on accepted markers, log_pfs, or other strata can induce selection artifacts, especially where feature_027 or feature_063 exposed cells are modest. I will accept only threshold-exceeding intervals with adequate cells and will not reinterpret accepted dataset-specific associations as causal or generalizable.",
  "research_notes": "Post-validation plan: core accepted associations are f037=1 stronger dependency; f010=1 and f002=1 weaker dependency; f027=1 stronger dependency (validated clean and robust in clean/full-clean contexts); f063=1 stronger dependency only in clean validated contexts, with broader/sparse variants not robust. Do not further refine f003 or broad f063 unless a new comparison has adequate cells and a lower bound clearly above 0.15.\n\nNew registered proposals: (1) complete low/high log_pfs stability for f027: low-logpfs triple-absent f027 (parent H97); high-logpfs f037 in f010=0/f002=0 (parent H65); high-logpfs f010 in f037=0/f002=0 (parent H66); high-logpfs f002 in f037=0/f010=0 (parent H67). (2) strict separability from f003: f063 in triple-absent/f027=0/f003=0 (parent H89), f027 in triple-absent/f063=0/f003=0 (parent H91). (3) two heterogeneity probes for f037: interaction with feature_038>=0.75 and interaction with feature_024 level_002 vs complement (parent H15). Existing H103-H109 remain active broad/reciprocal probes.\n\nInterpretation rules: accept only if signed estimate and 95% lower bound exceed 0.15 in claimed direction and cells are adequate; accept but defer sparse conditional estimates if lower bounds clear threshold but exposed n is modest; reject or leave unresolved near-threshold/failed-validation claims. All claims are dataset-specific observational associations, not causal or generalizable."
}

